    "title": "Dodgers win 8-4 in game two of series, officially ending Reds season - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Grace Erwin ,\n5 hours ago The Reds’ season is officially over after the Los Angeles Dodgers won the second game of the series 8-4 Wednesday.\nThe Dodgers took the first game Tuesday 10-5.\nZack Littell started on the mound for the Reds before he was replaced in the bottom of the fourth inning. Dodgers pitcher Yoshinobu Yamamoto had nine strikeouts throughout the game and was replaced in the seventh inning.\nThe Reds got on the board early in the first inning, when Gavin Lux and Austin Hays both scored after Sal Stewart singled on a ground ball. Stewart is the youngest Reds position player to appear in a postseason game in franchsie history.\nThe Reds remained up 2-0 until the third inning, when Ben Rortvedt scored for the Dodgers after a Mookie Betts single.\nHeading into the fourth inning, the Reds replaced Zack Littell with Nick Lodolo. The Dodgers brought the score up to 3-2 when Enrique Hernández doubled, allowing Max Muncy to score. Hernández then scored on a Miguel Rojas single on a fly ball.\nNeither team scored in the fifth inning, but the Dodgers cranked it up in the sixth. A single from Shohei Ohtani allowed Enrique Hernández to score. Directly after, Mookie Betts doubled and Ben Rortvedt scored.\nTeoscar Hernández then doubled, allowing Ohtani and Betts to score.\nBlake Treinen replaced Yoshinobu Yamamoto for the Dodgers in the seventh inning, giving up no runs. Mookie Betts doubled, leading Miguel Rojas to score, bringing the total up to 8-2.\nThe Reds ended the eighth inning with two more runs when Gavin Lux and Austin Hays both scored. The Dodgers finished out the eighth with no runs.\nThe Redlegs weren't able to score in the ninth inning, leaving the final score at 8-4 as their 2025 season came to an end.\nMore Reds news: Dodgers win 8-4 in game two of series, officially ending Reds season Dodgers take down Reds 10-5 in first game of series MLB spending disparity on display as $509M Dodgers take on $121M Reds",
